mask the issue with -timeout, test sharding outside the repository contract, cached pass results, or -run exclusions. Target cmd/curator <=480s on a clean unloaded -count=1 run, then require two consecutive foreground go test -count=1 ./... runs with the standard default timeout and real exit 0, plus focused global-status tests/coverage, build/vet/gofmt/diff checks, and disk before/after evidence. Do not clear shared Go caches, overlap the two decisive runs with another Go suite, install host software, stage, commit, publish, pin, or edit the separate TASK-260720-1nlmvv worktree. Attach exact optimization and gate evidence and hand off for independent review.\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: claude via explicit_override\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=claude; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\nspawn queued: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260729-3bd9db, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [implementer] developer (claude) (run=RUN-260729-3bd9db)\nDEVELOPER RUN-260729-3bd9db (performance rework): diagnostic complete, optimization NOT applied, blocked on an ownership/architecture decision. Artifact: TASK-260729-2kaopg_performance-diagnostic.md.\n\nCONSTRAINT: the cmd/curator aggregate duration is not caused by anything in the cmd/curator test setup. Bounded diagnostic go test -count=1 -timeout 30m -json ./cmd/curator exited 0 at 533.463s; 15 of 62 tests take 531.1s and every one of them drives a compiled installation. CPU profile of one compiled-install test attributes 8.24s of 13.18s samples (62.5%) to internal/godriver.fingerprintToolchain: os.(*Root).Open 3.61s, fs.WalkDir 3.26s (os.(*Root).Lstat 2.83s), copyWithContext only 1.34s. 84% of that hot path is os.Root path resolution, not reading or hashing. It walks and hashes the whole GOROOT (14502 files) once per read-only plan (godriver.Probe, deliberately never memoized), once per staging session (Establish), and three more times per staged build (VerifyToolchain at build.go:148/219/253 and plan.go:252). Measured atoms: one compiled-scope status = ~3.1s, one real compiled install = ~12.5s, the same scope without a compiled command = 1.08s for a full install plus four status calls.\n\nFAILED ASSUMPTION: the directive assumed repeated expensive setup inside cmd/curator. There is none left. Immutable setup is already shared - the two largest tests build one fixture and one installation and share them across 14 and 6 subtests - and the residual per-subtest cost is three CLI invocations that ARE the assertions (--json row fields, --check exit code, human line). Nothing is memoizable: the toolchain fingerprint is deliberately not, and the operation-private GOCACHE is a hermeticity property. Packaging cannot be split: the tests live in package main, call run() in-process, and assert on unexported identifiers, so a split requires moving run() into an importable package and rewriting ~1500 lines against string literals, which loses compile-time coupling and fails the equivalent-coverage condition. Trimming a task-owned GOROOT mirror is not viable either: src/ is 11006 of 14502 files and stdlib compilation needs it. No masking route (timeout, sharding, -run exclusion, cached results) was used.\n\nOPTION QUANTIFIED: a directory-scoped walk of fingerprintToolchain - descend through per-directory *os.Root handles and reuse the handle of the directory both phases are inside, keeping the identical record set, sort and byte stream - computes the IDENTICAL sha256 and is 2.2x faster (1.64s -> 0.72s per GOROOT, three rounds, identical_digest=true). Prototype in .temp/TASK-260729-2kaopg/probe/main.go; no worktree file was modified. Projected ~35% off cmd/curator (533s -> ~350s) plus material wins in internal/install (289s) and internal/install/atomicity (423s), which pay the same cost. Resolving each component once narrows rather than widens the TOCTOU window.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: option 1 - a separate godriver-owned task applies the change and re-attests the go-v1 conformance vectors, then the default gate is re-run here. Alternatives: option 2, authorize THIS task to make the production change under its own review; option 3, accept the duration and set an explicit package timeout, which the directive currently forbids.\n\nEXACT DECISION NEEDED: which of options 1/2/3, i.e. whether internal/godriver/fingerprint.go may be changed and by whom. internal/godriver is the go-v1 trust boundary and is outside this task declared surface (cmd/curator global status and its CLI tests), and the change moves the whole repository gate, so it was not taken unilaterally.\n\nGATES RUN THIS SESSION on the unchanged tree, real exit codes: gofmt -l . exit 0 no output; git diff --check exit 0; go build ./... exit 0; go vet ./... exit 0; go test -count=1 -coverprofile ./cmd/curator -run ^TestGlobalStatus exit 0 in 147.322s, package coverage 27.7%, disk avail 13784240 KiB before / 13799708 KiB after; go tool cover -func exit 0 with cmdGlobalStatus 96.9%, globalStatusPlan 100%, globalStatusScope 100%, statusReport 86.2%, classifySkillBuilds 81.8%, checkFailed/factsOf/Describe/plannedRows/markerMoved 100%. command -v golangci-lint exit 1, executable absent, not installed per directive.\n\nNOT RUN: the two consecutive default-timeout go test -count=1 ./... runs. Nothing in the tree changed, so they would reproduce the tester known exit 1 at the cmd/curator 10-minute package timeout; they are meaningless until the decision above is made. Host was not quiet: load average ~7.1 from unrelated processes and the data volume at 99% capacity; no run is reported as if taken on an idle host. Nothing staged, committed, published or pinned; the TASK-260720-1nlmvv worktree was not touched.\nagent completed: [implementer] developer (claude) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: claude (run=RUN-260729-3bd9db, pid=66385, exit=0)
